WEBVTT

00:00:06.882 --> 00:00:08.884
[radio static]

00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:13.722
[radio beeps]

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.807
[indistinct chatter over radio]

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.773
[narrator] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:42.167
<i>For the very first time,</i>

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:45.837
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:08.652
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.573
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.785
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:00.370
<i>Fresh water.</i>

00:02:01.747 --> 00:02:05.959
<i>Everything that lives on land,</i>
<i>animal or plant,</i>

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:07.961
<i>depends upon it.</i>

00:02:09.755 --> 00:02:13.759
<i>Yet only one percent of it</i>
<i>is within reach.</i>

00:02:33.195 --> 00:02:35.530
<i>The desert of central Australia.</i>

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:42.162
<i>One of the hottest, driest places</i>
<i>on Earth.</i>

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:49.544
<i>Virtually nothing can live here</i>
<i>for any length of time.</i>

00:02:52.464 --> 00:02:57.511
<i>Yet once a decade,</i>
<i>this vast, empty desert is transformed.</i>

00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:05.102
<i>For that to happen,</i>
<i>a chain reaction has to be triggered</i>

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.771
<i>two thousand kilometers to the north.</i>

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:19.491
<i>There, each year,</i>
<i>the moisture-laden clouds of the monsoon</i>

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:21.576
<i>begin to build.</i>

00:03:28.083 --> 00:03:30.085
[thunder rumbling]

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:39.386
<i>Eventually they burst,</i>
<i>and the process begins.</i>

00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:55.527
<i>Riverbeds that have been dry for years</i>
<i>suddenly fill.</i>

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.612
[birdsong]

00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:05.954
<i>The torrents join together</i>
<i>and race towards the interior</i>

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.165
<i>and the lowest land on the continent.</i>

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:15.881
<i>Here, they flood over the desert</i>
<i>and create Australia's largest lake,</i>

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:18.216
<i>Kati Thanda, Lake Eyre.</i>

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:29.060
<i>Huge numbers of fish</i>
<i>swim down these wide waterways</i>

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:31.062
<i>and into the growing lake.</i>

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:42.532
<i>Pelicans arrive from Australia's coasts,</i>
<i>hundreds of kilometers away.</i>

00:04:46.077 --> 00:04:50.707
<i>Many will only see the lake fill</i>
<i>once in their entire lives,</i>

00:04:51.625 --> 00:04:56.421
<i>so how they know the lake has formed,</i>
<i>and in which direction to reach it,</i>

00:04:56.505 --> 00:04:57.881
<i>is still a mystery.</i>

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:02.469
[pelicans cawing]

00:05:07.516 --> 00:05:13.230
<i>These new waters now contain so much food</i>
<i>that the birds start to breed.</i>

00:05:24.032 --> 00:05:26.034
<i>But the clock is ticking.</i>

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:30.747
<i>The pelicans feed...</i>

00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:33.124
<i>and feed...</i>

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:36.002
<i>and feed.</i>

00:05:38.839 --> 00:05:40.549
<i>The chicks must grow quickly,</i>

00:05:40.632 --> 00:05:44.177
<i>for they have to be strong enough</i>
<i>to leave within a matter of weeks.</i>

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:50.225
<i>But time is short,</i>

00:05:50.308 --> 00:05:52.936
<i>for the parents</i>
<i>will only be able to find food</i>

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:54.563
<i>while the water remains.</i>

00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:04.239
<i>The young pelicans set off</i>
<i>on their first long journey.</i>

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:11.162
<i>It will be a demanding one, to the coast,</i>

00:06:11.246 --> 00:06:14.082
<i>and the nearest is 500 kilometers away.</i>

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:19.504
<i>The lake will soon dry,</i>

00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:23.925
<i>and water may not return here</i>
<i>for another decade.</i>

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:34.477
<i>This boom to bust transformation</i>
<i>is an exceptional event.</i>

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:41.902
<i>But everywhere,</i>
<i>the availability of fresh water</i>

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:46.114
<i>is becoming increasingly unpredictable</i>
<i>and uncertain.</i>

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:04.090
<i>Much of the planet's fresh water, </i>
<i>however, is locked away.</i>

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:11.848
<i>Almost two-thirds of it</i>
<i>lies frozen around the poles.</i>

00:07:13.934 --> 00:07:17.354
<i>This ice is several kilometers thick</i>

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:21.316
<i>and may have been lying here</i>
<i>for millions of years.</i>

00:07:34.371 --> 00:07:36.623
<i>Every summer, some of it melts</i>

00:07:36.706 --> 00:07:39.918
<i>and carves its way</i>
<i>into the heart of the glaciers.</i>

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:48.677
<i>These great caves</i>
<i>are beautiful and romantic,</i>

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.765
<i>but they're also dangerous places,</i>
<i>for glaciers are always on the move.</i>

00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:56.267
[glacier creaking]

00:08:15.245 --> 00:08:17.247
<i>Even away from the poles,</i>

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.458
<i>much of the land's fresh water</i>
<i>freezes each winter.</i>

00:08:22.836 --> 00:08:28.049
<i>But when spring arrives,</i>
<i>this vast snowy reservoir melts</i>

00:08:28.133 --> 00:08:30.218
<i>and a great journey begins.</i>

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:39.227
<i>Racing down the steep mountain slopes,</i>

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:43.231
<i>the ice-cold waters</i>
<i>collect a life-giving ingredient...</i>

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:47.193
<i>oxygen.</i>

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:57.203
<i>Here, on the rivers of the Andes</i>
<i>in South America,</i>

00:08:57.787 --> 00:08:59.205
<i>there is a living to be made</i>

00:08:59.289 --> 00:09:02.250
<i>for those that can cope</i>
<i>with the powerful currents.</i>

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:05.336
<i>And torrent ducks can.</i>

00:09:10.842 --> 00:09:11.843
[ducks squawking]

00:09:16.973 --> 00:09:20.518
<i>These chilly headwaters</i>
<i>may be oxygen-rich,</i>

00:09:20.602 --> 00:09:22.896
<i>but they contain few nutrients.</i>

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:27.233
<i>The torrent ducks collect</i>
<i>what little food there is</i>

00:09:27.317 --> 00:09:29.235
<i>from below the surface.</i>

00:09:42.957 --> 00:09:46.795
<i>Stonefly and mayfly larvae have gills,</i>

00:09:46.878 --> 00:09:49.589
<i>with which they extract oxygen</i>
<i>from the water.</i>

00:09:53.051 --> 00:09:56.930
<i>Their flattened bodies</i>
<i>resist the relentless pull of the current.</i>

00:10:01.184 --> 00:10:02.560
<i>Black fly larvae.</i>

00:10:02.644 --> 00:10:04.395
<i>One of the ducks' favorite prey.</i>

00:10:06.064 --> 00:10:10.527
<i>Their specialized mouthparts</i>
<i>filter out the tiny particles of food</i>

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:11.694
<i>as they race by.</i>

00:10:13.988 --> 00:10:16.991
<i>Hooks at the rear end</i>
<i>anchor them to the rocks,</i>

00:10:17.534 --> 00:10:19.994
<i>but the current</i>
<i>is sometimes irresistible.</i>

00:10:26.251 --> 00:10:31.131
<i>However, they have a special safety line,</i>
<i>with which they can pull themselves back.</i>

00:10:46.604 --> 00:10:51.901
<i>Every year, visitors come</i>
<i>to these rushing, oxygen-rich waters.</i>

00:10:54.737 --> 00:10:55.572
<i>Salmon.</i>

00:10:57.949 --> 00:11:01.619
<i>They have spent most of their lives</i>
<i>in the Pacific Ocean.</i>

00:11:06.749 --> 00:11:11.629
<i>Now, fully grown, they're returning</i>
<i>to the particular rivers in North America</i>

00:11:11.713 --> 00:11:14.257
<i>where they will now lay their eggs.</i>

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.724
<i>Salmon have an extraordinary ability</i>
<i>to leap up waterfalls,</i>

00:11:24.434 --> 00:11:29.480
<i>but even they cannot, unaided,</i>
<i>cross the dams that we have built.</i>

00:11:34.068 --> 00:11:39.574
<i>Today, Pacific salmon number less than</i>
<i>one percent of the numbers they used to,</i>

00:11:39.657 --> 00:11:43.077
<i>and that's causing problems</i>
<i>for many other animals.</i>

00:11:48.458 --> 00:11:52.045
<i>Alaskan brown bears</i>
<i>wait for them at the waterfalls.</i>

00:11:59.427 --> 00:12:00.845
<i>Without these fish,</i>

00:12:00.929 --> 00:12:04.265
<i>the bears cannot build up</i>
<i>the fat reserves they need</i>

00:12:04.349 --> 00:12:07.894
<i>to sustain themselves</i>
<i>through their long winter hibernation.</i>

00:12:13.691 --> 00:12:17.445
<i>There is fierce competition</i>
<i>for the best fishing spots.</i>

00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:20.156
[growling]

00:12:43.179 --> 00:12:45.181
[gulls cawing]

00:12:55.024 --> 00:12:59.862
<i>Where water flows over hard rock,</i>
<i>it collects few nutrients,</i>

00:12:59.946 --> 00:13:02.031
<i>so few plants can grow in it.</i>

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:06.244
<i>Here, in South America, however,</i>

00:13:06.327 --> 00:13:09.956
<i>this plant, Macarenia,</i>
<i>manages to flourish.</i>

00:13:13.293 --> 00:13:15.128
<i>For a few months each year,</i>

00:13:15.211 --> 00:13:18.840
<i>when the water level is low enough</i>
<i>for sunlight to reach the bottom,</i>

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:21.175
<i>the Macarenia bursts into bloom.</i>

00:13:27.432 --> 00:13:31.894
<i>This is the Caño Cristales,</i>
<i>Colombia's rainbow river.</i>

00:13:41.738 --> 00:13:45.742
<i>Sometimes, however,</i>
<i>water disappears underground,</i>

00:13:45.825 --> 00:13:47.785
<i>beyond the reach of light,</i>

00:13:47.869 --> 00:13:50.663
<i>and there, no plants can live.</i>

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:58.880
<i>Thirty percent of the planet's fresh water</i>
<i>lies beneath the surface of the land,</i>

00:13:58.963 --> 00:14:01.883
<i>either within the substance</i>
<i>of porous rocks</i>

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:05.053
<i>or as subterranean lakes and rivers.</i>

00:14:08.681 --> 00:14:10.641
<i>Made slightly acid by rain,</i>

00:14:10.725 --> 00:14:14.854
<i>it slowly dissolves limestone,</i>
<i>creating great caves.</i>

00:14:16.939 --> 00:14:22.695
<i>A labyrinth of such passages and chambers</i>
<i>underlies much of the state of Florida.</i>

00:14:24.364 --> 00:14:26.491
<i>For the most part, they're barren.</i>

00:14:29.619 --> 00:14:33.373
<i>But where the water comes to the surface,</i>
<i>it's full of life.</i>

00:14:35.583 --> 00:14:37.251
<i>Fish are abundant.</i>

00:14:44.175 --> 00:14:46.177
<i>And so are fish eaters.</i>

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:48.805
<i>Otters.</i>

00:15:01.943 --> 00:15:05.530
<i>All over the state,</i>
<i>fresh water bubbles to the surface</i>

00:15:05.613 --> 00:15:09.283
<i>from the greatest concentration of springs</i>
<i>on Earth.</i>

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:32.181
<i>They feed rivers and pools,</i>
<i>in which all kinds of life flourish,</i>

00:15:32.265 --> 00:15:35.893
<i>including one of Florida's specialities...</i>

00:15:37.437 --> 00:15:38.521
<i>manatees.</i>

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.612
<i>Many spend the summer in the sea,</i>

00:15:47.196 --> 00:15:50.408
<i>but when winter approaches,</i>
<i>they swim up the rivers,</i>

00:15:50.491 --> 00:15:52.034
<i>which are relatively warm.</i>

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:04.881
<i>And there they graze</i>
<i>on an abundance of freshwater plants.</i>

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:14.265
<i>But human beings are now taking</i>
<i>so much water from these springs,</i>

00:16:14.348 --> 00:16:16.058
<i>and polluting many of the others,</i>

00:16:16.684 --> 00:16:19.687
<i>that the manatees</i>
<i>are losing their winter homes.</i>

00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:38.039
<i>Rivers, being comparatively shallow,</i>
<i>warm quickly,</i>

00:16:38.122 --> 00:16:40.208
<i>and in spring, across Europe,</i>

00:16:40.291 --> 00:16:44.378
<i>the rising temperatures</i>
<i>trigger a sudden surge of life.</i>

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:56.015
[tweets]

00:17:00.186 --> 00:17:05.149
<i>On Hungary's Tisza River,</i>
<i>giant mayfly are starting to emerge.</i>

00:17:11.697 --> 00:17:14.825
<i>For three years,</i>
<i>they've lived underwater as larvae.</i>

00:17:16.577 --> 00:17:18.996
<i>Now, in just three hours,</i>

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:24.585
<i>millions of the winged adults</i>
<i>dance together and mate before they die.</i>

00:17:41.143 --> 00:17:45.231
<i>This extraordinary spectacle</i>
<i>was once common in Eastern Europe,</i>

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:49.026
<i>but so many rivers</i>
<i>now have stabilized banks</i>

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.820
<i>and are badly polluted</i>

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:53.281
<i>that the mayflies</i>
<i>have largely disappeared.</i>

00:17:56.659 --> 00:18:00.955
<i>But when the rivers are clean,</i>
<i>wildlife is quick to return.</i>

00:18:23.352 --> 00:18:27.106
<i>The kingfisher,</i>
<i>the most glorious of divers.</i>

00:18:57.053 --> 00:19:00.848
<i>The osprey, the most skillful of graspers.</i>

00:19:39.303 --> 00:19:43.933
<i>Iguazu, on the frontier</i>
<i>between Brazil and Argentina.</i>

00:19:46.477 --> 00:19:49.980
<i>These are </i>
<i>the largest waterfalls on the planet.</i>

00:19:51.691 --> 00:19:56.612
<i>Thousands of tons of fresh water</i>
<i>thunder over them every second.</i>

00:20:06.956 --> 00:20:11.419
<i>Much of Iguazu's water</i>
<i>comes from the Amazon rainforest,</i>

00:20:11.502 --> 00:20:13.379
<i>a thousand kilometers away.</i>

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:26.684
<i>There, it rose as vapor</i>
<i>from the surface of the jungle canopy.</i>

00:20:28.519 --> 00:20:32.314
<i>A single tree can give off</i>
<i>a thousand liters a day.</i>

00:20:34.233 --> 00:20:39.739
<i>As the vapor rises into the sky,</i>
<i>it condenses into clouds.</i>

00:20:43.033 --> 00:20:46.912
<i>Twenty billion tons of water</i>
<i>leave the forest each day,</i>

00:20:46.996 --> 00:20:50.082
<i>more than travels</i>
<i>down the Amazon River itself.</i>

00:20:53.294 --> 00:20:55.629
<i>If the rainforest is destroyed,</i>

00:20:55.713 --> 00:20:58.257
<i>this life-giving cycle will be broken.</i>

00:21:00.301 --> 00:21:04.263
<i>As the clouds travel across the continent,</i>
<i>they shed their water,</i>

00:21:04.346 --> 00:21:07.016
<i>irrigating farmland and forest alike.</i>

00:21:08.267 --> 00:21:10.102
<i>And on the plains of Brazil,</i>

00:21:10.186 --> 00:21:13.898
<i>they create </i>
<i>the largest tropical wetland on Earth,</i>

00:21:14.857 --> 00:21:16.066
<i>the Pantanal.</i>

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:22.323
<i>Each year, however,</i>
<i>seasonal changes reduce the river's flow,</i>

00:21:22.406 --> 00:21:26.160
<i>and the Pantanal shrinks</i>
<i>to just a few river channels.</i>

00:21:31.373 --> 00:21:34.293
<i>This suits the top predator very well.</i>

00:21:38.255 --> 00:21:39.215
<i>The jaguar.</i>

00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:44.512
<i>It's a competent swimmer, but in water,</i>

00:21:44.595 --> 00:21:48.474
<i>it can't make the killing pounce</i>
<i>which makes it such a fearsome hunter.</i>

00:21:48.974 --> 00:21:50.267
[mewls]

00:21:53.896 --> 00:21:55.689
<i>Now, in the dry season,</i>

00:21:57.441 --> 00:21:59.735
<i>the banks can get somewhat crowded.</i>

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:09.203
<i>But the jaguar has spotted prey</i>
<i>on the river's edge.</i>

00:22:24.218 --> 00:22:25.052
<i>Capybara.</i>

00:22:28.556 --> 00:22:30.933
<i>It must get close to be successful.</i>

00:22:59.837 --> 00:23:04.174
<i>Two capybara incautiously</i>
<i>are standing in the shallows.</i>

00:23:15.853 --> 00:23:17.271
<i>They have seen one another.</i>

00:23:22.943 --> 00:23:24.320
<i>Which will run first?</i>

00:23:25.154 --> 00:23:26.113
[barks]

00:23:47.217 --> 00:23:52.056
<i>Farther along the river,</i>
<i>dense vegetation provides better cover,</i>

00:23:54.183 --> 00:23:56.727
<i>but the bank here is quite high.</i>

00:24:00.439 --> 00:24:02.900
<i>Capybara have acute hearing.</i>

00:24:05.861 --> 00:24:07.947
<i>-One noisy footfall...</i>
-[branch snaps]

00:24:09.239 --> 00:24:10.491
<i>...and the chance is lost.</i>

00:24:25.381 --> 00:24:26.632
<i>Another failure.</i>

00:24:57.246 --> 00:24:58.122
[barks]

00:25:10.509 --> 00:25:14.596
<i>Perhaps it's time to try a different prey.</i>

00:25:21.437 --> 00:25:23.564
<i>Tackling one of these would be risky,</i>

00:25:24.148 --> 00:25:26.775
<i>but the jaguar is really hungry.</i>

00:25:36.827 --> 00:25:38.871
<i>If a jaguar is to catch a caiman,</i>

00:25:38.954 --> 00:25:43.625
<i>it must bite its neck and then hold on</i>
<i>to prevent the caiman striking back.</i>

00:25:46.462 --> 00:25:50.507
<i>It has to get into a position</i>
<i>directly above its prey.</i>

00:25:56.805 --> 00:25:58.557
<i>This is dangerous.</i>

00:26:21.413 --> 00:26:24.583
<i>The caiman rolls</i>
<i>to try and drown the jaguar.</i>

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:32.633
<i>Predator and prey seem evenly matched.</i>

00:26:39.973 --> 00:26:41.934
<i>The jaguar won't let go.</i>

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:06.750
<i>After 20 minutes, and near to exhaustion,</i>
<i>the jaguar wins.</i>

00:27:17.094 --> 00:27:21.515
<i>Sometimes, a river's downward journey</i>
<i>is interrupted by lakes.</i>

00:27:22.808 --> 00:27:28.730
<i>Together, they contain 40 times more water</i>
<i>than all the world's rivers combined.</i>

00:27:31.942 --> 00:27:34.236
<i>Africa's Lake Tanganyika</i>

00:27:34.319 --> 00:27:39.158
<i>holds almost a fifth</i>
<i>of all accessible fresh water on Earth.</i>

00:27:44.246 --> 00:27:49.376
<i>But, considering its great size,</i>
<i>Tanganyika contains little life.</i>

00:27:55.549 --> 00:27:58.385
<i>It's almost a kilometer and a half deep,</i>

00:27:59.094 --> 00:28:03.724
<i>but at the bottom the water barely moves</i>
<i>and there is no oxygen.</i>

00:28:06.226 --> 00:28:11.064
<i>It's only close to the surface,</i>
<i>in its top 150 meters,</i>

00:28:11.148 --> 00:28:14.985
<i>that the water contains enough oxygen</i>
<i>for life to exist.</i>

00:28:17.821 --> 00:28:20.991
<i>But here, it flourishes in abundance.</i>

00:28:24.077 --> 00:28:28.290
<i>Two hundred and fifty species</i>
<i>of cichlid fish have evolved here,</i>

00:28:28.373 --> 00:28:30.542
<i>many of which are found nowhere else.</i>

00:28:40.761 --> 00:28:44.598
<i>Competition in these crowded waters</i>
<i>is intense.</i>

00:28:47.809 --> 00:28:51.730
<i>A male callipterus cichlid</i>
<i>collects shells.</i>

00:28:53.941 --> 00:28:58.278
<i>The more shells a male has,</i>
<i>the more females he will attract.</i>

00:29:02.991 --> 00:29:05.077
<i>And this one is doing very well.</i>

00:29:34.648 --> 00:29:37.776
<i>His neighbor has a rather smaller mound.</i>

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:45.993
<i>And the neighbor is a thief.</i>

00:29:52.124 --> 00:29:53.834
<i>As soon as he turns his back,</i>

00:29:57.087 --> 00:29:59.047
<i>the thief swoops in...</i>

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:03.385
<i>and steals a shell.</i>

00:30:14.187 --> 00:30:18.650
<i>The hardworking owner of the larger mound</i>
<i>doesn't seem to notice.</i>

00:30:21.403 --> 00:30:25.073
<i>So, the thief commits the crime again...</i>

00:30:26.992 --> 00:30:27.826
<i>and again...</i>

00:30:32.205 --> 00:30:35.667
<i>until he has built the largest mound.</i>

00:30:40.172 --> 00:30:42.716
<i>The shells are not just for display.</i>

00:30:42.799 --> 00:30:46.470
<i>They will be used by the females</i>
<i>as cradles.</i>

00:30:50.307 --> 00:30:51.767
<i>One arrives.</i>

00:30:52.392 --> 00:30:55.604
<i>She's tiny, only a tenth of his size.</i>

00:30:57.689 --> 00:31:01.276
<i>She's so small,</i>
<i>she can slip inside a shell.</i>

00:31:10.243 --> 00:31:15.332
<i>There, she lays her eggs,</i>
<i>and the male quickly fertilizes them.</i>

00:31:18.126 --> 00:31:23.423
<i>She will then stay inside protecting them</i>
<i>for up to a fortnight until they hatch.</i>

00:31:29.346 --> 00:31:30.347
<i>The emperor,</i>

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:33.058
<i>the largest of all the cichlids</i>
<i>in Tanganyika.</i>

00:31:35.977 --> 00:31:40.399
<i>At almost a meter long, this female</i>
<i>must raise her young in the open.</i>

00:31:46.321 --> 00:31:50.742
<i>Guarding them is therefore</i>
<i>a full-time job for both parents.</i>

00:32:00.127 --> 00:32:03.630
<i>But the future for all these fish</i>
<i>is uncertain.</i>

00:32:07.092 --> 00:32:11.388
<i>As global temperatures rise,</i>
<i>Lake Tanganyika is warming.</i>

00:32:13.432 --> 00:32:16.726
<i>Its waters are no longer mixing as well</i>
<i>as they once did,</i>

00:32:17.227 --> 00:32:20.564
<i>so the top oxygenated layer is reducing.</i>

00:32:31.074 --> 00:32:33.493
<i>The Mekong River in Southeast Asia.</i>

00:32:34.369 --> 00:32:39.207
<i>Four thousand kilometers long</i>
<i>and with the widest falls anywhere.</i>

00:32:43.128 --> 00:32:47.883
<i>Today, over 60 million people</i>
<i>depend upon it for their livelihood.</i>

00:33:11.114 --> 00:33:15.076
<i>The monsoon rains create</i>
<i>such a colossal surge of water</i>

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:17.787
<i>that the falls themselves</i>
<i>almost disappear.</i>

00:33:20.832 --> 00:33:23.084
<i>The water is loaded with sediment,</i>

00:33:23.168 --> 00:33:27.172
<i>collected during the Mekong's long journey</i>
<i>from the Tibetan Plateau.</i>

00:33:30.509 --> 00:33:35.222
<i>Even before the flood,</i>
<i>the water covers a vast area of land.</i>

00:33:41.353 --> 00:33:44.231
<i>These exposed roots are evidence</i>

00:33:44.314 --> 00:33:47.275
<i>of the great rise in water level</i>
<i>that is to come.</i>

00:33:52.781 --> 00:33:54.574
<i>When the main flood does arrive,</i>

00:33:54.658 --> 00:33:57.661
<i>the trees are submerged</i>
<i>up to their branches.</i>

00:34:02.541 --> 00:34:07.295
<i>For a few months, the floodwaters create</i>
<i>the planet's greatest breeding grounds</i>

00:34:07.379 --> 00:34:08.713
<i>for freshwater fish.</i>

00:34:12.676 --> 00:34:16.721
<i>One species</i>
<i>has a particularly ingenious solution</i>

00:34:16.805 --> 00:34:20.642
<i>to the problems of living</i>
<i>in shallow, oxygen-poor water.</i>

00:34:23.937 --> 00:34:26.147
<i>The Siamese fighting fish.</i>

00:34:31.611 --> 00:34:35.073
<i>The male breathes</i>
<i>by gulping air from the surface,</i>

00:34:35.156 --> 00:34:36.700
<i>and during the breeding season</i>

00:34:36.783 --> 00:34:40.704
<i>he uses that ability</i>
<i>to build a raft of bubbles.</i>

00:34:42.581 --> 00:34:45.166
<i>Day after day he labors.</i>

00:34:52.048 --> 00:34:56.886
<i>A female, waiting nearby,</i>
<i>rises to inspect the raft.</i>

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:16.906
<i>They wrap themselves around one another</i>
<i>in a tender embrace.</i>

00:35:26.875 --> 00:35:30.629
<i>This union encourages her</i>
<i>to release her eggs.</i>

00:35:33.632 --> 00:35:37.093
<i>As they sink downwards,</i>
<i>the male fertilizes them.</i>

00:35:42.932 --> 00:35:44.934
<i>Both carefully collect them,</i>

00:35:49.731 --> 00:35:54.903
<i>and then blow them, one by one,</i>
<i>into the bubble nest.</i>

00:35:58.865 --> 00:36:01.868
<i>Oxygen is scarce in these stagnant waters,</i>

00:36:01.951 --> 00:36:04.329
<i>but the eggs</i>
<i>will get enough from the bubbles</i>

00:36:04.412 --> 00:36:05.789
<i>to allow them to develop.</i>

00:36:08.458 --> 00:36:11.336
<i>The climax of the flood is still to come.</i>

00:36:14.005 --> 00:36:17.676
<i>Eventually,</i>
<i>so much water surges down the Mekong</i>

00:36:17.759 --> 00:36:23.390
<i>that the Tonlé Sap Lake expands</i>
<i>to five times its dry-season size.</i>

00:36:25.767 --> 00:36:30.438
<i>Vast numbers of fish now swim</i>
<i>into these new feeding grounds.</i>

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:36.277
<i>People have harvested this seasonal event</i>
<i>for millennia.</i>

00:36:37.821 --> 00:36:40.115
<i>They set nets across half of the river,</i>

00:36:40.615 --> 00:36:42.534
<i>leaving the other half free</i>

00:36:42.617 --> 00:36:45.453
<i>for the rest of the fish</i>
<i>to swim on and breed.</i>

00:36:49.541 --> 00:36:55.171
<i>The Mekong, in fact, supports</i>
<i>the largest inland fishery in the world.</i>

00:37:01.553 --> 00:37:06.015
<i>One-fifth of all the freshwater fish</i>
<i>caught by people worldwide</i>

00:37:06.516 --> 00:37:08.643
<i>comes from this one river system.</i>

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:13.064
<i>But the future of the Mekong,</i>

00:37:13.148 --> 00:37:15.650
<i>like that of so many other rivers</i>
<i>around the world,</i>

00:37:15.734 --> 00:37:17.694
<i>is increasingly at risk.</i>

00:37:21.114 --> 00:37:23.158
<i>We have changed the natural flow</i>

00:37:23.241 --> 00:37:26.786
<i>of more than two-thirds</i>
<i>of the planet's longest rivers</i>

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:30.999
<i>by, amongst other things,</i>
<i>building dams across them.</i>

00:37:39.507 --> 00:37:44.137
<i>So now, many rivers across the world</i>
<i>no longer flow.</i>

00:37:50.977 --> 00:37:54.898
<i>Here in East Africa, in Ruaha, Tanzania,</i>

00:37:55.857 --> 00:37:58.318
<i>these elephants are searching for water.</i>

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:08.077
<i>Each needs to drink 200 liters every day,</i>
<i>and there doesn't seem to be any here.</i>

00:38:09.704 --> 00:38:14.709
<i>But the elephants know that baobab wood</i>
<i>contains a lot of moisture.</i>

00:38:17.212 --> 00:38:21.216
<i>And in an emergency,</i>
<i>they eat it in great quantities.</i>

00:38:28.515 --> 00:38:31.100
<i>Lions also need water.</i>

00:38:35.772 --> 00:38:41.027
<i>And conveniently for the lions,</i>
<i>water is where their prey will gather.</i>

00:38:44.906 --> 00:38:48.868
<i>So both predators and prey</i>
<i>head for the rivers.</i>

00:38:54.833 --> 00:38:56.334
<i>Until 30 years ago,</i>

00:38:56.417 --> 00:38:59.337
<i>rivers in this part of Africa</i>
<i>never ran dry.</i>

00:39:04.717 --> 00:39:08.638
<i>Now, agriculture upstream</i>
<i>is taking great quantities,</i>

00:39:08.721 --> 00:39:13.351
<i>and during the dry season,</i>
<i>the rivers shrink into isolated pools.</i>

00:39:30.493 --> 00:39:36.207
<i>Hippos rely on flowing water</i>
<i>to keep themselves cool during the day,</i>

00:39:36.833 --> 00:39:40.211
<i>but now they have to make do with mud.</i>

00:39:44.465 --> 00:39:48.428
<i>As the water level falls still further,</i>
<i>they cram together.</i>

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:54.475
<i>Tempers begin to fray.</i>

00:40:03.902 --> 00:40:05.570
[roaring]

00:40:14.787 --> 00:40:20.710
<i>Each day, the mud pools shrink further,</i>
<i>and the hippos' plight worsens.</i>

00:40:33.806 --> 00:40:36.976
<i>Buffalo, in the urgent search for water.</i>

00:40:49.072 --> 00:40:52.951
<i>The lions are already here</i>
<i>at this water hole.</i>

00:41:01.834 --> 00:41:07.131
<i>However, the buffalo are driven on</i>
<i>by their desperate need for fresh water.</i>

00:41:20.937 --> 00:41:23.314
<i>They need to drink every day.</i>

00:41:38.788 --> 00:41:41.082
<i>Every drink is now a risk,</i>

00:41:42.834 --> 00:41:44.794
<i>but the buffalo have no choice.</i>

00:42:59.744 --> 00:43:03.748
<i>As the drought continues,</i>
<i>the buffalo begin to weaken.</i>

00:43:05.625 --> 00:43:08.586
<i>The odds are changing,</i>
<i>in the lions' favor.</i>

00:43:24.477 --> 00:43:27.188
<i>The elephants have reached their river.</i>

00:43:31.025 --> 00:43:35.113
<i>Nothing else is here because it's dry.</i>

00:43:47.208 --> 00:43:50.878
<i>But elephants can find water</i>
<i>where others can't.</i>

00:43:52.672 --> 00:43:57.051
<i>They use their trunks to dig holes</i>
<i>in the sand of the riverbed.</i>

00:44:06.561 --> 00:44:09.272
<i>The extraordinary sense of smell</i>

00:44:09.355 --> 00:44:13.067
<i>that enables them to detect open water</i>
<i>from kilometers away</i>

00:44:13.776 --> 00:44:17.405
<i>also helps them</i>
<i>to locate places underground</i>

00:44:17.488 --> 00:44:20.283
<i>where it lies closest to the surface.</i>

00:44:25.037 --> 00:44:29.292
<i>In severe droughts,</i>
<i>the wells they dig can be their lifeline.</i>

00:44:38.593 --> 00:44:42.471
<i>The hardships of the dry season</i>
<i>have always been part of life</i>

00:44:42.555 --> 00:44:44.307
<i>on East Africa's plains,</i>

00:44:45.183 --> 00:44:46.851
<i>but as the planet warms,</i>

00:44:46.934 --> 00:44:50.605
<i>and we ourselves take so much water</i>
<i>for our own purposes,</i>

00:44:51.189 --> 00:44:54.692
<i>the droughts are becoming more frequent</i>
<i>and more severe.</i>

00:45:08.998 --> 00:45:12.877
<i>The Platte River,</i>
<i>in Nebraska, North America.</i>

00:45:30.895 --> 00:45:36.359
<i>Each year, sandhill cranes arrive here</i>
<i>as they travel north across America</i>

00:45:36.442 --> 00:45:40.446
<i>on one of the last great migrations</i>
<i>made by any animal.</i>

00:45:42.782 --> 00:45:45.326
<i>It's one of their crucial staging posts.</i>

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:58.547
<i>The river once flooded the prairie</i>
<i>for a mile in all directions.</i>

00:46:02.134 --> 00:46:06.681
<i>This was a perfect place</i>
<i>for them to feed and rest.</i>

00:46:12.520 --> 00:46:14.981
<i>Now, we have dammed the river</i>

00:46:15.439 --> 00:46:18.484
<i>and taken so much of its water</i>
<i>for our own use</i>

00:46:19.026 --> 00:46:22.405
<i>that there is little space left</i>
<i>for the cranes.</i>

00:46:36.919 --> 00:46:41.507
<i>But conservationists</i>
<i>now manage the river's flow in such a way</i>

00:46:42.049 --> 00:46:45.803
<i>that it still creates the sandbanks</i>
<i>the cranes need.</i>

00:46:57.982 --> 00:47:00.985
<i>We are not alone in our need for water,</i>

00:47:06.949 --> 00:47:12.830
<i>but we have the ability to ensure</i>
<i>the fresh waters of the world do flow,</i>

00:47:14.915 --> 00:47:19.378
<i>and we alone can determine</i>
<i>how they are shared.</i>

00:47:29.889 --> 00:47:34.393
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>
<i>to discover what we need to do now</i>

00:47:34.477 --> 00:47:36.729
<i>to ensure fresh water keeps flowing.</i>

00:47:39.231 --> 00:47:45.154
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:47:47.782 --> 00:47:53.704
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:47:56.582 --> 00:48:00.836
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:48:00.920 --> 00:48:04.423
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:48:04.799 --> 00:48:10.888
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:48:10.971 --> 00:48:12.723
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>